#lang saffron
; saffron-dynamic-surface-pass M1: `.bind` on an `any`-held Option gave the
; wrong answer compiled -- `(type-of (half (some 7.1)))` was `unknown`, and the
; next `cast` panicked `any holds unknown, not Option` -- while `.fmap` on the
; same Option worked. Interpreted: 3.55, which is what this file now gets on
; both back ends.
;
; The difference between the two methods is one line of their DECLARATIONS:
;   Functor: (fmap [container g : (fn [a] b)]      : (m b))
;   Monad:   (bind [ma        k : (fn [a] (m b))]  : (m b))
; `g` returns a bare type variable; `k` returns the class variable APPLIED.
;
; The D8 witness cast every erased fn extra to `(fn [any] any)`. For `fmap`
; that is right and grounds `b := any`. For `bind` it cannot: `(m b)` does not
; unify with a bare `any`, so the result instantiation never grounded, `.bind`
; fell back to the ERASED CARRIER
; `__inst_Monad_bind_Option(int64_t, tur_poly_fn_t)`, and two things went wrong
; at once -- that body calls the continuation through
; `((int64_t (*)(void*, int64_t))k.fn)` while a Saffron lambda returns a
; 16-byte `tur_tagged_t`, dropping half the box on the return; and the `: any`
; pin then tagged an unresolved result with a bare TypeKind number, which is
; why `type-of` read `unknown`.
;
; So the filed fix direction -- "the witness's `: any` return must not re-widen
; a body that is already `any`" -- was wrong twice over: the body was not an
; `any`, and the defect was a truncation rather than a nesting.
;
; The witness now passes a MARSHALLING adaptor for such a continuation, the
; same move H7 makes at the argument seam:
;   (fn [__ka : any] : (Option any) (cast (__ak __ka) (Option any)))
; so `k` really is `(fn [any] (Option any))`, `b := any`, the instantiation is
; concrete, and the ABI scan mints the by-value spec.
;
; 7.1 and the halving, not a whole number: the element crosses two boxes and a
; closure, and the truncation this fixes printed a wrong number rather than
; failing loudly.

(defn half [o] (.bind o (fn [x] (some (/ x 2.0)))))

(defn main [] : int
  (let [r (half (some 7.1))]
    (println (type-of r))
    (println (cast (unwrap-or (cast r (Option any)) (:: 0.0 any)) float)))
  ; the None arm still short-circuits, and still types as an Option
  (println (type-of (half (none))))
  ; the Functor twin, which always worked -- pinned so a change to the witness
  ; cannot fix `bind` by breaking `fmap`
  (println (cast (unwrap-or (.fmap (some 7.1) (fn [x] (* x 2.0))) (:: 0.0 any)) float))
  0)
